IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `ei3-seat`

-- Worked cases for the seat layer ei3-seat.l4: one or more per question, in the facts a real person in the seat would state.
-- Seat: a person who has lost work or cannot work, or who is self-employed or a fisher, asking about Employment Insurance.
-- Every expected value is written by hand from the text of the section named in the question, never copied from the output.
-- All dates and amounts are invented for the cases; the premium rate 1.63 and the maximum 39,000 are test figures, not published ones.
-- Cases are numbered after the question numbers in the order of ei3-seat.l4.

§ `Premiums (Part III)`

-- Case A. Priya works for a bakery under a contract of service. Her employment is insurable, so she pays the employee's premium (s 67).
#ASSERT `Must I pay the employee's premium (s 67)` TRUE
-- Case B. Tomas is a company director paid no salary and his work is not insurable employment: he pays no employee's premium (s 67).
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I pay the employee's premium (s 67)` FALSE)
-- Case C. Priya has 40,000 of insurable earnings and the rate is 1.63%: 40,000 x 0.0163 = 652.
#ASSERT `How much employee's premium do I pay on my insurable earnings (s 67)` 40_000 1.63 EQUALS 652
#ASSERT `How much employee's premium do I pay on my insurable earnings (s 67)` 1_000 1.63 EQUALS 16.3
#ASSERT `How much employee's premium do I pay on my insurable earnings (s 67)` 0 1.63 EQUALS 0
-- Case D. A provincial plan pays Amélie sickness allowances that reduce her special benefits as an insured person: the employer's and employee's premiums (s 69(2)).
#ASSERT `Which premiums does a system for provincial plans reduce for me (s 69(2))` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST `ei3 the employer's and the employee's premiums`
-- Case E. Noor is a self-employed fisher and a provincial plan reduces her Part VII.1 benefits: the Part VII.1 premiums (s 69(2)).
#ASSERT `Which premiums does a system for provincial plans reduce for me (s 69(2))` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei3 the premiums under Part VII.1`
-- Case F. Both: all those premiums. No provincial plan pays: the subsection is silent (not covered).
#ASSERT `Which premiums does a system for provincial plans reduce for me (s 69(2))` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei3 all those premiums`
#ASSERT `Which premiums does a system for provincial plans reduce for me (s 69(2))` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case G. Priya did the work in 2025 but was paid on 5 January 2026: her earnings are treated as earned in 2026 (s 70). Paid in December 2025: 2025.
#ASSERT `In which year are my late-paid insurable earnings treated as earned (s 70)` 2025 (YMD 2026 1 5) EQUALS JUST 2026
#ASSERT `In which year are my late-paid insurable earnings treated as earned (s 70)` 2025 (YMD 2025 12 31) EQUALS JUST 2025
-- Paid before the year of the work: the section is silent.
#ASSERT `In which year are my late-paid insurable earnings treated as earned (s 70)` 2025 (YMD 2024 12 31) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case H. Priya's benefits are paid by special warrant (s 77(2)); money that is not benefits is not covered.
#ASSERT `Will my benefits be paid by special warrant (s 77(2))` TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei3 shall be paid by special warrants`
#ASSERT `Will my benefits be paid by special warrant (s 77(2))` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case I. A warrant issued electronically, or with the printed signatures, is validly issued; one that is neither is not.
#ASSERT `Is the special warrant I was given validly issued (s 77(2))` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is the special warrant I was given validly issued (s 77(2))` TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the special warrant I was given validly issued (s 77(2))` TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- Case J. Priya cashes her warrant at a credit union in Canada: negotiable without charge (s 77(3)). Abroad: not covered.
#ASSERT `Can I cash my special warrant without charge (s 77(3))` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Can I cash my special warrant without charge (s 77(3))` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Payment of Premiums (Part IV, ss 82 to 85)`

-- Case K. Her employer has paid Priya 38,000 so far this year, against a maximum of 39,000: it may still deduct (s 82(2)). At 39,000 it must stop.
#ASSERT NOT (`Must my employer stop deducting premium from my pay (s 82(2))` 38_000 39_000)
#ASSERT `Must my employer stop deducting premium from my pay (s 82(2))` 39_000 39_000
-- Case L. The employer missed Priya's premium on 10 March 2026 and deducts it from her pay of 10 March 2027, with one other missed premium: allowed (s 82(6)).
#ASSERT `May my employer deduct a premium it missed from my later pay (s 82(6))` (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2027 3 10) 1
-- A day later (more than 12 months), or two other missed premiums: not allowed.
#ASSERT NOT (`May my employer deduct a premium it missed from my later pay (s 82(6))` (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2027 3 11) 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`May my employer deduct a premium it missed from my later pay (s 82(6))` (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2026 9 1) 2)
-- Case M. Premium deducted on 15 May 2026 is treated as received by Priya on that day (s 82(7)).
#ASSERT `When am I treated as having received the premium my employer deducted (s 82(7))` TRUE (YMD 2026 5 15) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 5 15)
#ASSERT `When am I treated as having received the premium my employer deducted (s 82(7))` FALSE (YMD 2026 5 15) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case N. The employer wants Priya to bear its own premium: it cannot (s 84). For her own employee's premium the section says nothing.
#ASSERT `Can my employer make me bear its own premium (s 84)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can my employer make me bear its own premium (s 84)` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case O. A notice is put in Priya's secure electronic account; she authorized it; a message was sent on 2 July 2026 to the address she gave: presumed received 2 July 2026 (s 85(5)).
#ASSERT `When am I presumed to have received a notice put in my secure electronic account (s 85(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 7 2) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 7 2)
-- She had revoked that authorization first: no presumption.
#ASSERT `When am I presumed to have received a notice put in my secure electronic account (s 85(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 7 2) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Inspections, demands and protection (ss 88 and 89)`

-- Case P. An authorized person comes to Priya's bakery at 10 a.m. to examine the books: allowed (s 88(1)). At an unreasonable hour: not.
#ASSERT `May an authorized person inspect, audit or examine my documents (s 88(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May an authorized person inspect, audit or examine my documents (s 88(1))` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Case Q. Entering the bakery (not a dwelling-house) needs no consent or warrant; entering her flat does (s 88(1)(a), (2)).
#ASSERT `May an authorized person enter my premises (s 88(1)(a))` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`May an authorized person enter my premises (s 88(1)(a))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT `May an authorized person enter my premises (s 88(1)(a))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `May an authorized person enter my premises (s 88(1)(a))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT `Can an authorized person come into my home without a warrant (s 88(2))` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Can an authorized person come into my home without a warrant (s 88(2))` FALSE FALSE)
-- Case R. As the owner of the bakery Priya must help the authorized person and answer proper questions (s 88(1)(b)).
#ASSERT `Must I help an authorized person and answer their questions (s 88(1)(b))` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I help an authorized person and answer their questions (s 88(1)(b))` TRUE TRUE FALSE)
-- Case S. A judge asked ex parte, satisfied on all three grounds, may issue a warrant for Priya's flat (s 88(3)); not satisfied on necessity, no warrant but perhaps an access order (s 88(4)).
#ASSERT `May a judge issue a warrant to enter my home (s 88(3))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May a judge issue a warrant to enter my home (s 88(3))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT `May a judge order me to give access to documents kept in my home (s 88(4))` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May a judge order me to give access to documents kept in my home (s 88(4))` FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Case T. A notice served on Priya by registered mail asks for her invoices for a stated reasonable time, naming her: she must comply (s 88(5)).
#ASSERT `Must I give the Minister the information or document asked for in the notice (s 88(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- A notice asking her about unnamed customers without a judge's authorization is not a valid requirement; with the authorization it is.
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I give the Minister the information or document asked for in the notice (s 88(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Must I give the Minister the information or document asked for in the notice (s 88(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I give the Minister the information or document asked for in the notice (s 88(5))` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- Case U. A judge may authorize the Minister to ask Priya about unnamed persons only if all four conditions hold (s 88(7)); the authorization must be served with the notice (s 88(8)).
#ASSERT `May a judge authorize the Minister to ask me about unnamed persons (s 88(7))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May a judge authorize the Minister to ask me about unnamed persons (s 88(7))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Was the judge's authorization served on me together with the notice (s 88(8))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Was the judge's authorization served on me together with the notice (s 88(8))` TRUE FALSE)
-- Case V. Priya was served on 10 April 2026 and applies for a review on 25 April 2026, the 15th day: in time (s 88(9)); 26 April is not.
#ASSERT `May I apply to a judge to review the authorization served on me (s 88(9))` TRUE (YMD 2026 4 10) (YMD 2026 4 25)
#ASSERT NOT (`May I apply to a judge to review the authorization served on me (s 88(9))` TRUE (YMD 2026 4 10) (YMD 2026 4 26))
-- On the review the judge cancels if not satisfied the conditions are met, confirms or varies if satisfied (s 88(10)).
#ASSERT `May the judge cancel the authorization on my application for review (s 88(10))` FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the judge cancel the authorization on my application for review (s 88(10))` TRUE)
#ASSERT `May the judge confirm or vary the authorization on my application for review (s 88(10))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the judge confirm or vary the authorization on my application for review (s 88(10))` FALSE)
-- Case W. Priya is found guilty under s 106(2) of not complying with a s 88(5) requirement: the court may order compliance (s 88(11)).
#ASSERT `May the court order me to comply with the requirement after I am found guilty (s 88(11))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the court order me to comply with the requirement after I am found guilty (s 88(11))` FALSE)
-- Case X. The inspector may copy the invoices she showed (s 88(12)(a)).
#ASSERT `May a copy be made of the document I gave or showed (s 88(12)(a))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May a copy be made of the document I gave or showed (s 88(12)(a))` TRUE FALSE)
-- Case Y. Priya did everything s 88 required: she complied (s 88(13)). Obstructing the inspector: not.
#ASSERT `Have I complied with what section 88 requires of every person (s 88(13))` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Have I complied with what section 88 requires of every person (s 88(13))` FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I complied with what section 88 requires of every person (s 88(13))` TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I complied with what section 88 requires of every person (s 88(13))` FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Case Z. Priya cannot sue her employer for deducting premium in compliance with the Act (s 89(1)). A deduction not in compliance: the section is silent.
#ASSERT `Can I sue my employer for deducting premium from my pay (s 89(1))` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can I sue my employer for deducting premium from my pay (s 89(1))` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case AA. The employer owed Priya 1,000 and deducted premium as required; the Minister's receipt is for 800: 800 of its debt is discharged (s 89(2)); a 1,200 receipt discharges 1,000.
#ASSERT `How much of what my employer owes me does the Minister's receipt discharge (s 89(2))` TRUE TRUE 800 1_000 EQUALS JUST 800
#ASSERT `How much of what my employer owes me does the Minister's receipt discharge (s 89(2))` TRUE TRUE 1_200 1_000 EQUALS JUST 1_000
#ASSERT `How much of what my employer owes me does the Minister's receipt discharge (s 89(2))` TRUE FALSE 800 1_000 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Rulings and Appeals (ss 90 to 94)`

-- Case AB. Priya, an employee, asks the CRA whether her employment is insurable (s 90(1)); someone not named in the subsection is not covered.
#ASSERT `May I ask the Canada Revenue Agency for a ruling on this question (s 90(1))` `ei3 an employee` `ei3 whether an employment is insurable` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `May I ask the Canada Revenue Agency for a ruling on this question (s 90(1))` `ei3 a person claiming to be an employee` `ei3 how many hours an insured person has had in insurable employment` EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `May I ask the Canada Revenue Agency for a ruling on this question (s 90(1))` `ei3 someone not named in subsection 90(1)` `ei3 whether an employment is insurable` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `May I ask the Canada Revenue Agency for a ruling on this question (s 90(1))` `ei3 an employee` `ei3 a question not named in subsection 90(1)` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case AC. The question relates to 2025: Priya must ask before 30 June 2026 (s 90(2)): on 29 June yes, on 30 June no.
#ASSERT `Is my request for a ruling in time (s 90(2))` 2025 (YMD 2026 6 29)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my request for a ruling in time (s 90(2))` 2025 (YMD 2026 6 30))
-- Case AD. The officer ruled within a reasonable time (s 90(3)); not yet ruled: not.
#ASSERT `Has the officer ruled within a reasonable time (s 90(3))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Has the officer ruled within a reasonable time (s 90(3))` FALSE TRUE)
-- Case AE. Nobody asked for a ruling: the premium deducted from Priya's pay is treated as correctly deducted (s 90(4)(a)); a premium not deducted is treated as not required (s 90(4)(b)). If a ruling was asked for, neither deeming holds.
#ASSERT `If nobody asked for a ruling, is the premium deducted from my pay treated as correctly deducted (s 90(4)(a))` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`If nobody asked for a ruling, is the premium deducted from my pay treated as correctly deducted (s 90(4)(a))` TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT `If nobody asked for a ruling, is a premium that was not deducted from my pay treated as not required (s 90(4)(b))` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`If nobody asked for a ruling, is a premium that was not deducted from my pay treated as not required (s 90(4)(b))` TRUE TRUE)
-- Case AF. Priya's claim turns on whether her work was insurable: a CRA officer must rule (s 90.1). A question not in section 90, or one that does not arise in a claim: no.
#ASSERT `Must a Canada Revenue Agency officer rule on this question in my claim (s 90.1)` `ei3 whether an employment is insurable` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must a Canada Revenue Agency officer rule on this question in my claim (s 90.1)` `ei3 whether an employment is insurable` FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Must a Canada Revenue Agency officer rule on this question in my claim (s 90.1)` `ei3 a question not named in subsection 90(1)` TRUE)
-- Case AG. Priya, a person concerned, was notified of a ruling on 1 March 2026 and appeals on 30 May 2026, the 90th day: in time (s 91); 31 May is not; a stranger cannot appeal.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal to the Minister from the ruling in time (s 91)` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 5 30)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to the Minister from the ruling in time (s 91)` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 5 31))
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to the Minister from the ruling in time (s 91)` FALSE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 3 2))
-- Case AH. An appeal that may affect Priya: she is owed notice and a hearing (s 93(1)); the Minister did both or did not.
#ASSERT `Am I owed notice and a chance to be heard on an appeal that may affect me (s 93(1))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I owed notice and a chance to be heard on an appeal that may affect me (s 93(1))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `Did the Minister notify me and give me the chance to be heard (s 93(1))` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Did the Minister notify me and give me the chance to be heard (s 93(1))` TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Did the Minister notify me and give me the chance to be heard (s 93(1))` TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- Case AI. The appeal went to the Assistant Director of Appeals at a Tax Services Office and was mailed there (s 93(2)).
#ASSERT `Have I sent my appeal to the right place (s 93(2))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I sent my appeal to the right place (s 93(2))` TRUE FALSE)
-- Case AJ. The Minister decided in a reasonable time and told her (s 93(3)); and may notify her in any manner the Minister considers adequate (s 93(4)).
#ASSERT `Has the Minister decided my appeal in a reasonable time and told me (s 93(3))` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Has the Minister decided my appeal in a reasonable time and told me (s 93(3))` TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `May the Minister notify me of an appeal in a manner of their choosing (s 93(4))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Minister notify me of an appeal in a manner of their choosing (s 93(4))` TRUE FALSE)
-- Case AK. The Minister decides on their own initiative although nobody asked for a ruling: sections 90 to 93 do not restrict that (s 94).
#ASSERT `Can the Minister decide on their own even though I asked for no ruling (s 94)` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can the Minister decide on their own even though I asked for no ruling (s 94)` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Overpayments and Refunds (ss 95 and 96)`

-- Case AL. Two employers each paid Priya 30,000 (total 60,000) and deducted 978 in all at 1.63%, against a maximum of 39,000: the overpayment is 978 - 0.0163 x 39,000 = 342.30 (s 95).
#ASSERT `How much of the premium deducted from my pay is an overpayment (s 95)` 978 60_000 39_000 1.63 EQUALS 342.3
-- One employer only, 30,000 of earnings: nothing in excess.
#ASSERT `How much of the premium deducted from my pay is an overpayment (s 95)` 489 30_000 39_000 1.63 EQUALS 0
-- Case AM. Priya applies in writing on 30 June 2025 for her 342.30 overpayment from 2023: the Minister shall refund it (s 96(1)); on 1 January 2027 it is too late (three years after the end of 2023 is 31 December 2026).
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund my overpayment or premium paid when I was not insured (s 96(1))` TRUE FALSE 342.3 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 6 30) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 342.3
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund my overpayment or premium paid when I was not insured (s 96(1))` FALSE TRUE 75 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2026 12 31) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 75
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund my overpayment or premium paid when I was not insured (s 96(1))` TRUE FALSE 342.3 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2027 1 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund my overpayment or premium paid when I was not insured (s 96(1))` TRUE FALSE 342.3 FALSE 2023 (YMD 2025 6 30) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
-- Case AN. An appeal decision of 1 March 2026 found 80 too much premium was taken; Priya applies on 31 March 2026, the 30th day: refund of 80 (s 96(2)); on 1 April, none.
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund me after an appeal decision that too much premium was taken (s 96(2))` TRUE TRUE 80 TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 3 31) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 80
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund me after an appeal decision that too much premium was taken (s 96(2))` TRUE TRUE 80 TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 4 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
-- Case AO. The Minister MAY refund what should not have been deducted, if Priya satisfies the Minister and applies in three years (s 96(3)).
#ASSERT `May the Minister refund me an amount deducted that should not have been (s 96(3))` TRUE TRUE 2023 (YMD 2026 12 31)
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Minister refund me an amount deducted that should not have been (s 96(3))` TRUE FALSE 2023 (YMD 2025 1 1))
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Minister refund me an amount deducted that should not have been (s 96(3))` TRUE TRUE 2023 (YMD 2027 1 1))
-- Case AP. Dev earned 1,500 in 2023 from two short jobs, and 24.45 of premium was deducted: the whole 24.45 is refunded (s 96(4)). With 2,001 of earnings, no.
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund all the premium taken from me on earnings of $2,000 or less (s 96(4))` 1_500 24.45 FALSE 0 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 24.45
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund all the premium taken from me on earnings of $2,000 or less (s 96(4))` 2_001 32.6 FALSE 0 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
-- Dev is also self-employed with combined earnings of 2,500: barred (s 96(4.1)); at exactly 2,000 combined, not barred.
#ASSERT `Must the Minister refund all the premium taken from me on earnings of $2,000 or less (s 96(4))` 1_500 24.45 TRUE 2_500 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
#ASSERT `Am I barred from the s 96(4) refund because I am also self-employed (s 96(4.1))` TRUE 2_500
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I barred from the s 96(4) refund because I am also self-employed (s 96(4.1))` TRUE 2_000)
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I barred from the s 96(4) refund because I am also self-employed (s 96(4.1))` FALSE 9_000)
-- Case AQ. Insurable earnings of 2,100 and 150 deducted: 2,100 - 150 = 1,950, under 2,000 by 50, so the refund is 50 (s 96(5)). A self-employed person is under (5.1) instead.
#ASSERT `How much will the Minister refund if my earnings after premium fall under $2,000 (s 96(5))` FALSE 2_100 150 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 50
#ASSERT `How much will the Minister refund if my earnings after premium fall under $2,000 (s 96(5))` TRUE 2_100 150 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
-- Case AR. Noor, also self-employed: total earnings 2,500, deducted 100, Part VII.1 premium 450: TE - TP = 1,950, (b) is 50, lesser of 100 and 50 is 50 (s 96(5.1)).
#ASSERT `How much will the Minister refund if I am also self-employed and my combined earnings after premium fall under $2,000 (s 96(5.1))` TRUE TRUE 2_500 100 450 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 50
#ASSERT `How much will the Minister refund if I am also self-employed and my combined earnings after premium fall under $2,000 (s 96(5.1))` TRUE TRUE 2_500 30 600 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 a refund is payable` 30
#ASSERT `How much will the Minister refund if I am also self-employed and my combined earnings after premium fall under $2,000 (s 96(5.1))` TRUE TRUE 3_000 60 500 TRUE 2023 (YMD 2025 3 1) EQUALS `ei3 no refund is payable under the provision`
-- Case AS. An application for the 2023 premium made in writing on 31 December 2026 is in time; one made a day later is not (s 96(10)).
#ASSERT `Have I applied for my refund in time (s 96(10))` TRUE 2023 (YMD 2026 12 31)
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I applied for my refund in time (s 96(10))` TRUE 2023 (YMD 2027 1 1))
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I applied for my refund in time (s 96(10))` FALSE 2023 (YMD 2025 1 1))
-- Case AT. The Minister refunded Priya 500 when 300 was due: the excess 200 may be recovered from her as a debt (s 96(11)).
#ASSERT `Must I repay a refund the Minister gave me by mistake (s 96(11))` TRUE 500 300
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I repay a refund the Minister gave me by mistake (s 96(11))` TRUE 300 300)
#ASSERT `How much of a refund must I repay (s 96(11))` 500 300 EQUALS 200
#ASSERT `How much of a refund must I repay (s 96(11))` 300 300 EQUALS 0
-- Case AU. Priya owes Her Majesty a payment and a refund is otherwise payable: the Minister may keep it (s 96(12)).
#ASSERT `May the Minister keep my refund to pay what I owe Her Majesty (s 96(12))` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Minister keep my refund to pay what I owe Her Majesty (s 96(12))` TRUE FALSE)
-- Case AV. Interest of 12.50 at the prescribed rate on a refunded overpayment is paid (s 96(13)); interest under $1 is not.
#ASSERT `Will I be paid interest on my refund (s 96(13))` TRUE 12.5
#ASSERT `Will I be paid interest on my refund (s 96(13))` TRUE 1
#ASSERT NOT (`Will I be paid interest on my refund (s 96(13))` TRUE 0.99)
#ASSERT NOT (`Will I be paid interest on my refund (s 96(13))` FALSE 50)

§ `Administration (ss 97 to 102)`

-- Case AW. Priya pays a Part IV penalty with a cheque to the Receiver General: the bank must take it without charge (s 100). A cheque to someone else: no.
#ASSERT `Must a bank take my cheque to the Receiver General without charge (s 100)` TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must a bank take my cheque to the Receiver General without charge (s 100)` FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Case AX. An RCMP member may lay an information (s 102(1)); a private citizen may not. Priya may not challenge the informant's authority; the Minister may.
#ASSERT `May this person lay an information or complaint against me under Part IV (s 102(1))` FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`May this person lay an information or complaint against me under Part IV (s 102(1))` FALSE FALSE FALSE)
#ASSERT NOT (`May I challenge the informant's authority to lay the information (s 102(1))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `May I challenge the informant's authority to lay the information (s 102(1))` TRUE
-- Case AY. An objection that the information covers two or more offences fails (s 102(2)); other objections are not covered.
#ASSERT `Can I object that the information covers two or more offences (s 102(2))` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can I object that the information covers two or more offences (s 102(2))` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case AZ. Priya lives within the judge's territory though the matter arose elsewhere: the judge may try her (s 102(3)).
#ASSERT `May a provincial court judge try me although the matter arose elsewhere (s 102(3))` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`May a provincial court judge try me although the matter arose elsewhere (s 102(3))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Case BA. The matter arose on 15 March 2020: a charge laid on 15 March 2025 is in time (s 102(4)); on 16 March 2025 it is not.
#ASSERT `Can I still be charged with an offence under Part IV (s 102(4))` (YMD 2020 3 15) (YMD 2025 3 15)
#ASSERT NOT (`Can I still be charged with an offence under Part IV (s 102(4))` (YMD 2020 3 15) (YMD 2025 3 16))
-- Case BB. An officer's affidavit about a registered letter, with all its parts, is evidence it was sent (s 102(5)); missing the exhibits, not.
#ASSERT `Does an officer's affidavit prove a request, notice or demand was mailed to me (s 102(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does an officer's affidavit prove a request, notice or demand was mailed to me (s 102(5))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `Does an officer's affidavit prove a request, notice or demand was served on me personally (s 102(6))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does an officer's affidavit prove a request, notice or demand was served on me personally (s 102(6))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- Case BC. An officer's affidavit that after careful search no return of Priya's was found is evidence she did not file (s 102(7)); the day-of-filing affidavit proves that day (s 102(8)).
#ASSERT `Is an officer's affidavit that no return of mine was found evidence I did not file one (s 102(7))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is an officer's affidavit that no return of mine was found evidence I did not file one (s 102(7))` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE)
#ASSERT `Does an officer's affidavit prove the day I filed a return (s 102(8))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does an officer's affidavit prove the day I filed a return (s 102(8))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
-- Case BD. The other side need not prove the officer's signature or status (s 102(11)), but the subsection says nothing of other matters.
#ASSERT `Must the other side prove the officer's signature or status when offering an affidavit (s 102(11))` TRUE `ei3 the signature of the person making the affidavit` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the other side prove the officer's signature or status when offering an affidavit (s 102(11))` TRUE `ei3 that the person making the affidavit is an officer of the Canada Revenue Agency` EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the other side prove the officer's signature or status when offering an affidavit (s 102(11))` TRUE `ei3 any other matter` EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Must the other side prove the officer's signature or status when offering an affidavit (s 102(11))` FALSE `ei3 the signature of the person making the affidavit` EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case BE. Judicial notice is taken of an order made under Part IV: Priya need not plead or prove it (s 102(12)).
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I plead or prove an order made under Part IV (s 102(12))` TRUE)
#ASSERT `Must I plead or prove an order made under Part IV (s 102(12))` FALSE
-- Case BF. A notice over the Minister's written name is treated as signed and issued by the Minister (s 102(13)), unless the Minister calls it into question.
#ASSERT `Is a document over the Minister's name treated as signed and issued by the Minister (s 102(13))` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is a document over the Minister's name treated as signed and issued by the Minister (s 102(13))` TRUE TRUE TRUE)
-- Case BG. A form that looks authorized by the Minister is treated as authorized (s 102(15)).
#ASSERT `Is a form that looks authorized by the Minister treated as authorized (s 102(15))` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is a form that looks authorized by the Minister treated as authorized (s 102(15))` TRUE TRUE)
-- Case BH. In a prosecution, a return that appears to be Priya's is proof she filed it unless there is evidence to the contrary (s 102(16)).
#ASSERT `Does producing a return that appears to be mine prove I filed it (s 102(16))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does producing a return that appears to be mine prove I filed it (s 102(16))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE)
-- Case BI. A notice sent to a general partnership at its latest known address is treated as given to each partner (s 102(19)(b)); to a limited partnership's limited partner only: not.
#ASSERT `Is a notice sent to my partnership treated as given to me as a partner (s 102(19)(b))` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is a notice sent to my partnership treated as given to me as a partner (s 102(19)(b))` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is a notice sent to my partnership treated as given to me as a partner (s 102(19)(b))` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is a notice sent to my partnership treated as given to me as a partner (s 102(19)(b))` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE)

§ `Objection and Review (ss 103 to 105)`

-- Case BJ. The Minister's decision was communicated to Priya on 1 March 2026. She appeals on 30 May 2026, the 90th day: in time (s 103(1)); on 31 May she needs the Court's leave.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal to the Tax Court of Canada in time (s 103(1))` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 5 30) NOTHING NOTHING
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to the Tax Court of Canada in time (s 103(1))` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 5 31) NOTHING NOTHING)
-- She applied on 15 June for more time and the Court allowed her until 30 September 2026: an appeal on 1 July is in time.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal to the Tax Court of Canada in time (s 103(1))` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 7 1) (JUST (YMD 2026 6 15)) (JUST (YMD 2026 9 30))
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to the Tax Court of Canada in time (s 103(1))` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 10 1) (JUST (YMD 2026 6 15)) (JUST (YMD 2026 9 30)))
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to the Tax Court of Canada in time (s 103(1))` FALSE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 3 2) NOTHING NOTHING)
-- Case BK. The Court may vacate, confirm or vary (s 103(3)(a)); an appeal under s 92 may be sent back for reassessment (s 103(3)(b)); decision and reasons (s 103(3)(c), (d)).
#ASSERT `May the Tax Court vacate, confirm or vary the decision I appealed (s 103(3)(a))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Tax Court vacate, confirm or vary the decision I appealed (s 103(3)(a))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `May the Tax Court send my assessment back to the Minister for reassessment (s 103(3)(b))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Tax Court send my assessment back to the Minister for reassessment (s 103(3)(b))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `Must the Tax Court tell me its decision in writing (s 103(3)(c))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must the Tax Court tell me its decision in writing (s 103(3)(c))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `Must the Tax Court's reasons be in writing (s 103(3)(d))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must the Tax Court's reasons be in writing (s 103(3)(d))` FALSE)
-- Case BL. The Tax Court may decide whether Priya is affected (s 104(1)).
#ASSERT `May the Tax Court or the Minister decide this question in my appeal (s 104(1))` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `May the Tax Court or the Minister decide this question in my appeal (s 104(1))` FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`May the Tax Court or the Minister decide this question in my appeal (s 104(1))` FALSE FALSE)
-- Case BM. The Tax Court's decision binds Priya unless the Act provides otherwise (s 104(2)).
#ASSERT `Is the decision or ruling final and binding on me (s 104(2))` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the decision or ruling final and binding on me (s 104(2))` TRUE TRUE)
-- Case BN. The Court asked Priya to attend and she did: she is owed the allowances the Treasury Board approved, here 250 (s 104(3)).
#ASSERT `Am I owed allowances for attending the Tax Court (s 104(3))` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I owed allowances for attending the Tax Court (s 104(3))` TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE)
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I owed allowances for attending the Tax Court (s 104(3))` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE)
#ASSERT `How much are my allowances for attending the Tax Court (s 104(3))` TRUE 250 EQUALS JUST 250
#ASSERT `How much are my allowances for attending the Tax Court (s 104(3))` FALSE 250 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Case BO. The Court's decision on a section 103 appeal cannot be appealed except under the Federal Courts Act (s 105): otherwise JUST FALSE; the Federal Courts Act appeal is outside the section.
#ASSERT `Can I appeal or seek review of the Tax Court's decision on my section 103 appeal (s 105)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can I appeal or seek review of the Tax Court's decision on my section 103 appeal (s 105)` TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Offences (ss 106 and 107)`

-- Case BP. Priya refuses to give the authorized person the assistance section 88 requires: she commits an offence (s 106(2)); if she contravenes nothing, none.
#ASSERT `Do I commit an offence by contravening section 88 (s 106(2))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Do I commit an offence by contravening section 88 (s 106(2))` FALSE)
-- Case BQ. Contravening an information-return regulation is an offence (s 106(3)); the least fine for 10 days of default is 250; for 41 days the $1,000 ceiling prevails.
#ASSERT `Do I commit an offence by contravening an information-return regulation (s 106(3))` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Do I commit an offence by contravening an information-return regulation (s 106(3))` FALSE)
#ASSERT `What is the least fine for my days of default (s 106(3))` 10 EQUALS 250
#ASSERT `What is the least fine for my days of default (s 106(3))` 41 EQUALS 1_000
-- Case BR. Noor files a Part VII.1 return with a false statement: an offence (s 106(4)); with none of the five acts, no offence.
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence by false statements or evading premium (s 106(4))` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence by false statements or evading premium (s 106(4))` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Have I committed an offence by false statements or evading premium (s 106(4))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Have I committed an offence by false statements or evading premium (s 106(4))` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE)
-- Case BS. Noor was fined 1,000 plus 200 by reference to a 100 premium sought to be evaded, with no jail: within s 106(5). A fine of 24.99, or an addition of 200.01, is not.
#ASSERT `Is the punishment I was given within what section 106(5) allows (s 106(5))` 1_000 200 100 0
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the punishment I was given within what section 106(5) allows (s 106(5))` 24.99 0 100 0)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the punishment I was given within what section 106(5) allows (s 106(5))` 1_000 200.01 100 0)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the punishment I was given within what section 106(5) allows (s 106(5))` 1_000 0 100 7)

§ `Regulations (s 108)`

-- Case BT. A regulation may prescribe for 4 days of not filing an information return the greater of $100 and $25 a day (100); for 5 days 125; for 101 days the $2,500 maximum (s 108(1)(c)).
#ASSERT `What penalty may a regulation prescribe for my days of not filing a return (s 108(1)(c))` 4 EQUALS 100
#ASSERT `What penalty may a regulation prescribe for my days of not filing a return (s 108(1)(c))` 5 EQUALS 125
#ASSERT `What penalty may a regulation prescribe for my days of not filing a return (s 108(1)(c))` 101 EQUALS 2_500

§ `Pilot projects (Part V)`

-- Case BU. A pilot-project regulation that applied to Noor came into force on 15 March 2026: it ceases to have effect on 15 March 2029 (s 110); on 14 March 2029 it still has effect; a repealed regulation is outside the section.
#ASSERT `Has the pilot-project regulation that applied to me ceased to have effect (s 110)` FALSE (YMD 2026 3 15) (YMD 2029 3 15) EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Has the pilot-project regulation that applied to me ceased to have effect (s 110)` FALSE (YMD 2026 3 15) (YMD 2029 3 14) EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Has the pilot-project regulation that applied to me ceased to have effect (s 110)` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 15) (YMD 2030 1 1) EQUALS NOTHING
